BKS = "This lesson summarises ideas from The Body Keeps the Score by Bessel van der Kolk in our own words. It is for general understanding only and is not therapy, diagnosis or treatment advice. If this topic brings up difficult feelings, please consider speaking with a qualified professional."
MS = "This lesson summarises ideas from Mindset by Carol S. Dweck in our own words."
L = {}
L['bks-mind-body'] = dict(
intro="The Body Keeps the Score is a widely read book about trauma, written by a psychiatrist who has worked with trauma survivors for many years. Its central idea is that overwhelming experiences can affect not only how we think and feel, but also how our bodies respond. This lesson introduces that idea gently and in general terms. " + BKS,
sections=[
("The book's central idea", "The book argues that experiences which overwhelm a person's ability to cope can leave lasting effects that show up in the body as well as the mind. A person might know, rationally, that they are safe, yet their body may still react as if danger is present. The title expresses this idea: the body can carry the imprint of past experiences, even when the mind would rather move on."),
("Mind and body are connected", "Many everyday experiences show that mind and body are linked. A stressful meeting can bring a tight stomach. Worry can make it hard to sleep. Feeling calm can relax the shoulders. The book builds on this ordinary observation and explores how, after very stressful experiences, these links can become stuck in patterns that are hard to change through thinking alone."),
("Why understanding helps", "For some readers, the most valuable part of the book is recognising that certain reactions, such as feeling on edge, numb or easily startled, may be understandable responses rather than personal failings. This understanding can bring relief and reduce self-blame. However, a book cannot assess any individual's situation. Only a qualified professional can help someone understand their own experiences and what support might suit them."),
("Paying gentle attention to the body", "In everyday life, simply noticing bodily sensations, such as tension, breath or warmth, can be part of general self-awareness. This is different from therapy. If paying attention to your body brings up distress or memories that feel overwhelming, it is best to stop and seek support from a professional rather than push through alone."),
("Reading with care", "Because the book discusses painful experiences in detail, some readers find parts of it upsetting. It is perfectly fine to read slowly, skip sections, or put it down. Looking after yourself while learning matters more than finishing a chapter."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: a racing heart after a near-miss", "Imagine someone in Delhi who narrowly avoids a road accident. For days afterwards, the sound of a horn makes their heart race, even when they are safely indoors. Their mind knows they are fine, but their body reacts quickly. Many people experience short-lived reactions like this after a frightening event, and they often ease with time and support from loved ones. When reactions are intense or last a long time, talking to a qualified professional is the right step. This is an illustrative scenario, not a description of any condition or a guide to self-assessment."),
takeaways=["The book's central idea is that overwhelming experiences can affect both mind and body.","Mind and body are linked in many everyday ways.","Understanding can reduce self-blame, but only a professional can assess an individual situation.","Read and reflect at your own pace, and seek support if difficult feelings arise."],
reflect=["Where in your body do you notice everyday stress, such as a busy day or a tense conversation?","Who could you reach out to if a topic like this ever felt heavy?"],
)
L['bks-connection'] = dict(
intro="Feeling safe with other people is one of the most important foundations for wellbeing. The Body Keeps the Score gives considerable attention to relationships and to the sense of safety that comes from connection. This lesson explores, in general terms, why connection matters and how it relates to feeling secure. " + BKS,
sections=[
("Safety and connection", "The book emphasises that humans are social beings, and that feeling safe with others is deeply connected to wellbeing. When we feel seen, heard and accepted, our bodies and minds often settle. When we feel threatened, alone or misunderstood, it can be much harder to relax. The book suggests that relationships play a central role in how people recover from overwhelming experiences."),
("Why connection can feel hard", "For some people, especially after painful experiences, being close to others can feel risky rather than comforting. Trust may have been broken. Crowds or intimacy may feel overwhelming. The book acknowledges that this is understandable and that rebuilding a sense of safety with others can take time. There is no need to force closeness; small, safe moments of connection can be a gentle start."),
("Small moments of attunement", "Connection does not have to mean deep conversations. A shared laugh, a friendly greeting, a walk with a neighbour, or playing music together can all create moments of feeling in tune with someone. The book discusses activities that involve doing things together in rhythm, such as singing or moving, as ways people sometimes reconnect. These are general observations, not prescriptions."),
("Being a safe person for others", "We can also help others feel safe. Listening without rushing to fix or judge, keeping promises, respecting boundaries, and staying calm when someone is upset all contribute to a sense of safety. These everyday qualities can matter a great deal to people who are going through a hard time."),
("Knowing when to seek help", "Friends and family are valuable, but they are not a replacement for professional support when it is needed. If difficulties with trust or closeness are causing significant distress, a qualified professional can help in ways a friend cannot. Seeking help is a sign of care for yourself, not weakness."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: the evening walking group", "After moving to a new city for work, Rekha felt isolated and on edge in her small flat in Ahmedabad. A neighbour invited her to join a group of women who walked in the local park each evening. At first she barely spoke, but the regular, easy rhythm of walking alongside others, chatting about small things, helped her feel less alone. Over time, the park walk became a steady, comforting part of her week. This is an illustrative story, not a description of any treatment."),
takeaways=["The book highlights how feeling safe with others supports wellbeing.","After painful experiences, closeness can feel risky; small safe moments can help.","Connection can come through shared activities, not only conversation.","Friends matter, but professional support is important when difficulties are significant."],
reflect=["With whom do you feel most at ease, and what about them helps?","How could you be a safe, steady presence for someone this week?"],
)
L['bks-many-paths'] = dict(
intro="The Body Keeps the Score does not present a single solution. Instead, it discusses a range of approaches that people and professionals have explored in recovering from trauma. This lesson gives a general, high-level overview of that idea of 'many paths'. It does not recommend any approach for any individual. " + BKS,
sections=[
("No single path", "One of the book's messages is that recovery can look different for different people. What helps one person may not suit another. The book discusses several kinds of approaches, including talk-based therapies, body-based practices, and creative or group activities. Its broader point is that because overwhelming experiences can affect both body and mind, approaches that involve both are worth considering alongside traditional talk."),
("Body-based practices", "The book discusses practices that involve the body, such as yoga and other forms of mindful movement, in the context of trauma recovery. In general wellness terms, many people find gentle movement and breathing calming. But in the context of trauma, the book describes these within a broader approach, often guided by trained professionals. They are not a do-it-yourself treatment."),
("Creative and community activities", "The book also discusses creative and shared activities, such as theatre, music and group programmes, as ways people have explored expressing feelings and reconnecting with others. These ideas highlight that healing is not only something that happens in a therapist's office; community and creativity can play roles too."),
("Professional therapies", "The book describes various professional therapies, some of which are specialised and require trained practitioners. Some of these are discussed as still being studied and debated. This lesson does not describe how any therapy works or whether it would be suitable. Only a qualified professional can advise on what might help a particular person, and that conversation is the right place to explore options."),
("Hope without pressure", "The idea of many paths can be hopeful: if one approach does not help, others may. It also removes the pressure to find a single perfect answer. Recovery is often gradual, with ups and downs, and seeking support is a strength."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: different paths, same direction", "Consider two friends in Chennai who each went through a very hard time. One found that talking regularly with a counsellor helped her make sense of things. The other found that, alongside professional support, a gentle community yoga class and singing in a choir helped him feel more settled in his body. Neither path was 'right' or 'wrong'; each was guided by their own needs and by professional advice. This is an illustrative scenario, not a recommendation of any treatment."),
takeaways=["The book discusses many approaches rather than one solution.","These include talk-based, body-based, creative and community approaches.","Professional therapies require trained practitioners and individual advice.","If one path does not help, others may; seek guidance from a qualified professional."],
reflect=["What activities generally help you feel calm and settled in your body?","If you or someone you know needed support, where could you find a qualified professional?"],
)
L['bks-self-compassion'] = dict(
intro="After hard experiences, many people judge their own reactions harshly: 'Why can't I just get over it?' or 'Something must be wrong with me.' The Body Keeps the Score offers a different perspective: many responses to overwhelming stress are understandable attempts by mind and body to cope. This lesson explores how that understanding can support self-compassion. " + BKS,
sections=[
("Reactions that make sense", "The book describes how, under overwhelming stress, people's bodies and minds respond in ways meant to protect them. Some may feel on high alert. Others may feel shut down or numb. These responses, the book suggests, are not signs of weakness or character flaws. They are understandable reactions to experiences that were too much to handle at the time."),
("Replacing blame with understanding", "If you believe your reactions mean something is wrong with you, you may add shame on top of distress. Understanding that the reactions have a protective origin can soften that shame. Instead of 'What's wrong with me?', the question can become 'What happened to me, and what do I need now?' This shift does not erase pain, but it can make it easier to treat yourself with patience."),
("What self-compassion looks like", "Self-compassion means treating yourself with the kindness you would offer a good friend who was struggling. In practice, it might mean speaking to yourself gently, resting when you are exhausted, or acknowledging that something was hard instead of dismissing it. It is not self-pity or making excuses. It is a steady, caring stance towards your own experience."),
("Self-compassion in daily life", "Small acts can express self-compassion: placing a hand on your chest when you feel distressed, taking a short walk when overwhelmed, or reminding yourself that many people feel this way. These are general wellbeing practices, not treatments. If distress is persistent or intense, a qualified professional can offer support that self-help alone cannot."),
("Compassion for others too", "Understanding that reactions to stress make sense can also change how we see others. Someone who seems irritable, withdrawn or jumpy may be coping with more than we know. Responding with patience rather than judgement can make a meaningful difference."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: changing the inner voice", "Farhan, a college student in Hyderabad, went through a stressful family situation and found himself unable to concentrate on his studies. He kept telling himself he was lazy. A friend gently pointed out that anyone would struggle after what he had been through. Farhan began, slowly, to replace 'I'm lazy' with 'This has been hard, and I'm doing what I can.' He also spoke to his college counsellor, who helped him plan support. This is an illustrative story, not a description of any treatment."),
takeaways=["Many reactions to overwhelming stress are understandable protective responses.","Shifting from 'What's wrong with me?' to 'What do I need?' can reduce shame.","Self-compassion means treating yourself as you would a struggling friend.","For persistent or intense distress, seek a qualified professional."],
reflect=["What harsh phrase do you sometimes say to yourself? How might a kind friend rephrase it?","How might understanding someone else's stress change how you respond to them?"],
)
L['ms-two-mindsets'] = dict(
intro="Why do some people shy away from challenges while others seek them out? Mindset, by psychologist Carol S. Dweck, suggests that much depends on what we believe about our abilities. The book describes two mindsets, fixed and growth, and how they shape learning, effort and resilience. This lesson introduces both. " + MS,
sections=[
("The fixed mindset", "In a fixed mindset, a person believes that abilities such as intelligence or talent are set traits: you have a certain amount, and that is that. If you believe this, every challenge becomes a test of how much you have. Success proves you are smart; failure suggests you are not. Naturally, this can make people avoid challenges, hide mistakes and feel threatened by others' success."),
("The growth mindset", "In a growth mindset, a person believes that abilities can be developed through effort, good strategies and help from others. This does not mean anyone can become anything, or that everyone is equal in every skill. It means that wherever you start, you can grow. With this belief, challenges become opportunities to learn, mistakes become information, and effort becomes the path to improvement."),
("Mindsets are not fixed labels", "The book emphasises that people are not purely one or the other. Most of us have a mix, and our mindset can differ by area. You might have a growth mindset about cooking but a fixed mindset about maths. Certain situations, such as being criticised or seeing someone better than you, can trigger fixed-mindset thinking. Recognising these triggers is part of moving towards growth."),
("Why it matters", "The book suggests that mindset influences how people respond to difficulty. Someone with a fixed mindset may give up when things get hard, because difficulty feels like evidence of inadequacy. Someone with a growth mindset may persist, because difficulty is a normal part of learning. Over time, these different responses can lead to different outcomes."),
("Starting to shift", "Moving towards a growth mindset begins with noticing your inner voice. When you hear 'I'm just not good at this', you can add 'yet'. When you feel threatened by someone's success, you can ask what you might learn from them. These small shifts, practised over time, can change how you approach challenges."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: 'I'm not a maths person'", "Neha, a class 10 student in Bhubaneswar, had always told herself she was 'not a maths person'. When she struggled with algebra, she stopped trying, believing it was pointless. Her teacher encouraged her to see mistakes as clues and to try different methods. Neha began saying, 'I don't understand this yet.' She did not suddenly love maths, but she became willing to keep working at it, and her understanding improved steadily. This is an illustrative story."),
takeaways=["A fixed mindset sees abilities as set; a growth mindset sees them as developable.","Mindset shapes how we respond to challenges, mistakes and effort.","Most people have a mix of mindsets that varies by area and situation.","Adding 'yet' to 'I can't do this' is a simple start."],
reflect=["In which area of life do you most often think 'I'm just not good at this'?","Which situations tend to trigger fixed-mindset thinking for you?"],
)
